# [What Yamux is]($heading.id('what'))

[Yamux](https://github.com/hashicorp/yamux/blob/master/spec.md) ("yet
another multiplexer") is a small protocol from HashiCorp that carries many
logical streams over one ordered byte stream. libp2p uses it too. Each piece
of data is sent as a *frame* with a 12-byte header that names its stream, so
frames from different streams can be interleaved and sorted out at the other
end.

Without it, two devices would need one TCP connection and one Noise handshake
for each request in flight, or would have to wait for one request to finish
before starting the next. With it, a large block transfer and a directory
listing can share the connection without one waiting for the other.

# [Frames on the wire]($heading.id('frames'))

[Streams for three requests, interleaved as frames on one connection, and the header every frame starts with.](<$image.siteAsset('learn/yamux.svg').alt('Three streams at the top, stream 1 for GET_BLOCK, stream 3 for GET_SHARES and stream 5 for GET_SWARM, feed one pipe where their frames are interleaved: stream 1 SYN, stream 3 SYN, stream 1 DATA, stream 5 SYN, stream 3 DATA, stream 1 FIN, all on one Noise session over one TCP connection. Below, the 12-byte frame header: version 1 byte, type 1 byte, flags 2 bytes, stream ID 4 bytes, length 4 bytes. Types are DATA, WINDOW_UPDATE, PING and GO_AWAY; flags SYN opens, FIN ends, RST resets; length is payload bytes or window credit. 256 KiB window per stream, 64 streams at most.')>)

Every frame starts with the same header, integers big-endian:

| Field | Size | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| version | 1 B | always 0 |
| type | 1 B | `DATA`, `WINDOW_UPDATE`, `PING` or `GO_AWAY` |
| flags | 2 B | `SYN` opens a stream, `FIN` ends one side, `RST` aborts, `ACK` marks a ping reply |
| stream ID | 4 B | which stream the frame belongs to |
| length | 4 B | bytes of payload after the header, or the window credit granted |

Yamux sits inside [Noise]($link.page('learn/noise')): a Yamux frame is
written to the Noise stream, which seals it in encrypted frames of at most
1024 bytes. The other side decrypts, reads the header, and appends the
payload to the right stream's buffer. Unknown versions or frame types are
rejected.

# [Opening and closing streams]($heading.id('streams'))

Both sides can open streams. To keep their IDs from colliding, the side that
dialed the connection uses odd IDs (1, 3, 5, …) and the side that accepted it
uses even IDs (2, 4, …). A stream opens with a `DATA` frame carrying `SYN`
and no payload. The other side queues it until something accepts it.

Closing is per direction. `FIN` means "I won't send more", and the stream is
freed once both sides have closed it. `RST` drops it at once. `GO_AWAY` asks
the peer to open no new streams; streams already open keep working.

# [Flow control]($heading.id('flow'))

Each stream has a receive *window* of 256 KiB: the most the sender may send
before the receiver has read it. A writer that has used up the window waits.
When the application reads from a stream, the receiver sends a
`WINDOW_UPDATE` frame granting that many bytes back. A slow reader on one
stream holds back only that stream's sender.

A session allows at most 64 live streams. Together with the window, that
limits the data a peer can make a device buffer for it to 16 MiB per
connection.

# [How NomadFS uses streams]($heading.id('nomadfs'))

Every request gets its own stream: open, send one message, read one reply,
close. A `PING`, a `GET_BLOCK` for one chunk, a `GET_SHARES` or a `GET_SWARM`
each run on their own stream with their own timeout. On the receiving side,
one thread accepts streams and hands each to a handler thread. A request that
takes long, such as reading a megabyte from disk, doesn't hold up the others.

Each connection also runs one reader thread that pulls frames off the Noise
stream and sorts them into streams.

Source: `src/net/transport/yamux.zig`, `src/net/transport/tcp.zig`,
`src/net/discovery/node.zig`.
